<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1">
  <POU Name="fb_Permissive" Id="{9a75ca4f-f276-450d-a1f6-8310b103476b}" SpecialFunc="None">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(*
    fb_Permissive — a set of up to TEN conditions that must ALL be true for something to be
    ALLOWED to proceed. All-true means satisfied, and unwired slots default TRUE so you wire only
    what applies.

    SELF-CONTAINED, per CONVENTIONS §5: ten is a literal here, in ST_Permissive's array bounds and
    in the loops below — this FB references no GVL and reads no global state. Everything it needs
    arrives through inpConfig. Raising the capacity means editing those together.

    PERMISSIVE vs INTERLOCK — the distinction that decides which one you want:
      * an INTERLOCK (fb_Interlock) is a condition that must hold for a device to KEEP RUNNING.
        Breaking one is abnormal: it stops the device and it is a FAULT the operator must clear.
      * a PERMISSIVE is a condition that must be met for something to START or ADVANCE. Not being
        met yet is NORMAL — the machine is waiting, not broken. "Chamber not yet at vacuum" is not
        a fault, it is a Tuesday.
    So this FB deliberately raises NO fault, has no TcAlarm plumbing and does not extend
    abs_fb_Device. It answers exactly one question — "may this proceed, and if not, which conditions
    are not met".

    USAGE
        VAR
            PermStart : fb_Permissive;
        END_VAR

        // describe the slots once (startup or FB_Init of the owner)
        PermStart.inpConfig.descriptions[1] := 'Chamber door closed';
        PermStart.inpConfig.descriptions[2] := 'Vacuum below 5 Torr';
        PermStart.cfgName := 'Start permissive';

        // drive them every scan; leave unused slots alone (they stay TRUE)
        PermStart.inpConfig.conditions[1] := DoorClosed;
        PermStart.inpConfig.conditions[2] := Pressure < 5.0;
        PermStart();

        IF PermStart.stsPermissiveOK THEN ... END_IF

    NO FIRST-OUT, and that is the sharpest line between this and fb_Interlock. An interlock TRIPS:
    one condition drops, the device faults instantly, and "which one went first" names the cause -
    the later ones are usually consequences. A permissive never trips. It is simply evaluated at the
    moment something asks to proceed, and every unmet condition is equally and simultaneously the
    reason. There is no first, so the FB publishes the SET (stsConditions + stsUnmetCount) and the
    HMI shows all of them rather than nominating a misleading headline.

    NO BYPASS, either. It is tempting to allow one on the grounds that a permissive was never a
    safety function — but a bypass is precisely the control that makes an unsatisfied gate report
    itself satisfied, and a sequence advanced on a forced permissive fails later, somewhere that
    explains nothing. The conditions hold or they do not. If a condition needs to not apply, do not
    wire that slot; it defaults TRUE. (fb_Interlock's inpGlobalBypass is a genuinely different
    case: an interlock STOPS a running machine, so commissioning needs a documented way past it.)

    It is a DISPLAY-and-SEQUENCING aid, not a safety function: real safety lives in the safety PLC /
    hard wiring, and an interlock that must stop a running device belongs in fb_Interlock.

    Consumers: abs_fb_MachineFlow transition gates — the machine ANDs stsPermissiveOK into
    setPermissive(t, ...); MachineFlow holds no reference to this FB. Also device start conditions and
    the shared lhmi PermissiveList component (binds .HMI directly).
*)
FUNCTION_BLOCK fb_Permissive
VAR_INPUT
    inpConfig   : ST_Permissive;
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    stsPermissiveOK : BOOL;     // TRUE when every condition is met
    stsUnmetCount   : INT;      // number of unmet conditions
END_VAR

VAR
    i   : INT;
    HMI : ST_Permissive_HMI_Interface;
END_VAR]]></Declaration>
    <Implementation>
      <ST><![CDATA[// Count every unmet slot every scan. The count is wanted even while satisfied, so there is no
// early exit.
//
// NO FIRST-OUT HERE, deliberately - see the header block. First-out is a TEMPORAL question and a
// permissive has no temporal dimension: nothing tripped, so no condition "went first". Every unmet
// condition is equally and simultaneously the reason, which is why the consumer shows the SET.
//
// 10 is a literal, matching ST_Permissive's array bound - CONVENTIONS §5. fb_Interlock's bound and
// its loop can drift apart for exactly this reason; keep these two edits together.
stsUnmetCount := 0;

FOR i := 1 TO 10 DO
    IF NOT inpConfig.conditions[i] THEN
        stsUnmetCount := stsUnmetCount + 1;
    END_IF
END_FOR

stsPermissiveOK := (stsUnmetCount = 0);

// ── publish to HMI (last section, per CONVENTIONS §3) ──
HMI.stsPermissiveOK := stsPermissiveOK;
HMI.stsConditions   := inpConfig.conditions;
HMI.stsDescriptions := inpConfig.descriptions;
HMI.stsUnmetCount   := stsUnmetCount;]]></ST>
    </Implementation>
    <Method Name="getHMIdata" Id="{b0c19f6f-4dd0-4508-a580-da645616bc13}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[METHOD PUBLIC getHMIdata : ST_Permissive_HMI_Interface
VAR_INPUT
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[getHMIdata := HMI;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Method Name="setCondition" Id="{d93931d8-4abc-4f37-8d18-a2a48c3396f2}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Drive one slot, optionally naming it at the same time. Convenience over poking inpConfig
// directly: it bounds-checks the slot, so a typo'd index is a no-op instead of a memory scribble,
// and it keeps a condition and its description together at the call site where they are easiest to
// keep in step. Pass an empty inpDescription to leave the existing text alone.
METHOD PUBLIC setCondition : BOOL
VAR_INPUT
    inpSlot         : INT;
    inpMet          : BOOL;
    inpDescription  : STRING;
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
      <Implementation>
        <ST><![CDATA[IF inpSlot < 1 OR inpSlot > 10 THEN
    setCondition := FALSE;
    RETURN;
END_IF

inpConfig.conditions[inpSlot] := inpMet;
IF inpDescription <> '' THEN
    inpConfig.descriptions[inpSlot] := inpDescription;
END_IF
setCondition := TRUE;]]></ST>
      </Implementation>
    </Method>
    <Property Name="cfgName" Id="{ee53dbe9-a04c-4c73-bca3-e20a825a5fce}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[// Operator-facing name for this permissive set, shown as the heading in the HMI list.
PROPERTY PUBLIC cfgName : STRING]]></Declaration>
      <Get Name="Get" Id="{eba1314a-471b-451b-9511-ecf37989341c}">
        <Declaration><![CDATA[VAR
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
        <Implementation>
          <ST><![CDATA[cfgName := HMI.stsPermissiveName;]]></ST>
        </Implementation>
      </Get>
      <Set Name="Set" Id="{87926a5a-80fe-43c5-943a-98e5228cd559}">
        <Declaration><![CDATA[VAR
END_VAR
]]></Declaration>
        <Implementation>
          <ST><![CDATA[HMI.stsPermissiveName := cfgName;]]></ST>
        </Implementation>
      </Set>
    </Property>
  </POU>
</TcPlcObject>
